  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA weak area of low pressure tracks through Quebec tonight, \nbringing a weak cold front through the region. Showers and \nstorms reach into northwestern locations late this afternoon and\nevening ahead of the front. The best chance for these will be \ncloser to the Canadian border and the low pressure center, with \ngradually more scattered activity further south and toward the \ncoast. A stronger gusty storm can't be ruled out through \nnorthern New Hampshire early this evening, but otherwise the \nconvection is expect to weaken and decay as it moves eastward \nthrough the late evening and into the overnight hours.\n\nAny lingering showers are expected to move offshore by dawn \ntomorrow, with clearing conditions to follow. Areas that do see \nsome rain this evening stand the chance to see some patchy fog \ndevelop by morning, with the best chance being through the \nnorthern valleys.\n\nMainly sunny skies are expected south of the mountains \ntomorrow, with a few more clouds and some isolated pop up \nshowers through the higher terrain. The airmass continues to \nslowly and incrementally cool with each passing front, yielding\nhighs in the mid 70s across the north, to mid 80s along the \ncoast and through southern NH tomorrow. Dew points will also \nfall back into the 50s to low 60s, keeping the airmass much more\ncomfortable overall.\n\nWith the drier airmass, low temperatures fall into the 50s \ntomorrow night in most spots, with some upper 40s possible \nacross the northern valleys. Lows around 60 degrees are more \nlikely through southern New Hampshire where some mid to high \nlevel clouds keep temps a little warmer most of the night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA broad mid-level trough will engulf the northeast by Wednesday \nmorning. The trough should allow for cooler, more average \ntemperatures to emerge over the 2nd half of the week.\nProbabilistic ensemble guidance has been a bit cooler than the \nmost recent run of the NBM. Opted to nudge high temperatures \ndown on Wed/Thurs by 2-3F. It should be fairly difficult for any\nlocation in the area to exceed 85F Wedensday through Friday. \nTemperatures should stay cooler through the upcoming weekend.\n\nRegarding precipitation, low pressure looks to move across the \nregion at some point Thursday or Friday. Guidance is in agreement \nthat a trough axis swings into the area and will likely strengthen \nwith upper-level jet stream dynamics as it exits the northeast. \nDuring its passage, rain, thunderstorms are likely and could be more \nwidespread. However, there will not be much moisture for the storm. \nPWATs look to be in the range of 1.1-1.5 inches - which is quite dry \nwith low pressure overhead.  With this in mind, rainfall amounts \ndon't look like anything special nor impactful.\n\nIn addition, we will need to keep an eye on the high tides for \nThursday. Astronomical tides are high for the month, and the tide \ngauges will be close to reaching minor flood stage. Light \noffshore winds and very low wave heights should prevent gauges CASM1\n(Portland) and HPMN3 (Hampton) from reaching minor flood stage.\nImpacts should be minimal, but some high water along the coast \nis likely.",
